"""serverjack: a phone-friendly web front door to your home server.

    https://<host>/        -> this app (default 127.0.0.1:7680)
    https://<host>/term/   -> the terminal, reverse-proxied by this app to
                              ttyd on <runtime dir>/ttyd.sock

Everything it does ends in the same place: a command running inside a tmux
session, with a terminal on your screen. The landing page has three panels,
in the order you actually need them from a phone:

  1. Start a session -- a segmented Shell / <agent> choice, a directory
     picker (default ~), and, for Shell only, an optional command box: an
     agent told you to run `sudo apt install ffmpeg` and can't do that
     itself, so paste it and tap Start -- it runs in front of a login shell,
     so a sudo password prompt works and the output stays on screen
     afterwards. Picking an agent instead just runs its plain command
     (`claude`, `codex`, ...) in the chosen directory; there is no
     remote-control/server-mode choice here, that lives in Agent servers
     below.
  2. Sessions        -- list, open, kill.
  3. Agent servers    -- an accordion with one collapsed row per coding CLI
     that still needs something: not installed, installed but not logged in,
     or ready with a server, daemon or extra action to offer. A tool that is
     ready with nothing else to configure (just Gemini CLI, by default) has
     no row here -- it only ever needed the picker at the top. Open one (they
     are exclusive, native <details name=...>, no JS) and the body offers one
     option row per way of running it in the background -- a remote-control
     server, a daemon, a pairing action -- each with a one-line note on the
     difference. Every button just launches a command in a session and shows
     you the terminal -- install scripts print progress, login flows print a
     URL or a device code, remote-control servers print a QR code. serverjack
     never parses what a tool prints. The one exception is the Codex daemon,
     whose start/stop are quick non-interactive commands.

Opening a session goes to /s/<name>: a slim menu bar (session tabs, + new,
x close, pop-out, soft keys for phones) over the terminal in a frame at
/term/?arg=<name>. That path is served by this process, which forwards it --
WebSocket upgrade and all -- to ttyd on a Unix socket; ttyd runs
bin/tmux-attach.sh with that argument, which attaches. The terminal only ever appears once you are inside a session. When a
session has more than one tmux window the active tab carries a "2/3" badge and
tapping it opens a window list.

Routes
    GET  /                     the landing page
    GET  /s/<name>             the terminal wrapper for one session
    GET  /app.js               the wrapper's script
    GET  /manifest.webmanifest, /icon.svg, /icon-180.png     PWA bits
    GET  /healthz              "ok"
    GET  /api/sessions         [{name, windows, attached, ...}]
    GET  /api/windows?name=    [{index, name, active, cmd}]
    GET  /api/tools            one state object per agent
    GET  /api/screen?name=     the session's pane as plain text
    GET  /api/status           counts + agent + machine summary, for a dashboard
    POST /start                the Start a session card: a shell (with an
                               optional command) or an agent, in one form
    POST /new, /api/new        create a session -- kept for the terminal
                               page's own new-session form; the landing page
                               posts to /start instead
    POST /kill, /api/kill      kill one
    POST /rename, /api/rename  rename one
    POST /api/window           select a tmux window
    POST /run                  legacy alias for /start's "shell + command"
                               case, kept working for anything still using it
    POST /shortcuts/add, /shortcuts/del, /shortcuts/run
    POST /tools (dispatches to /tools/install, /tools/login, /tools/open,
         /tools/action, /tools/server, /tools/daemon, /tools/autostart)

Renaming a session leaves a browser sitting on /s/<old> without a session.
That is harmless: the page polls /api/sessions every 15s, sees the old name is
gone, and moves itself to another session -- the same path a killed session
takes.

Config files, all in $SERVERJACK_CONFIG (default ~/.config/serverjack):
    env             read by systemd, not by this file
    tools.json      agent registry overrides (see below)
    shortcuts.json  saved one-click commands
    autostart.json  [{"tool": id, "kind": "server"|"daemon", "dir": path}] --
                    servers/daemons to bring up shortly after serverjack starts

Stdlib only, no dependencies. Runs as your user and talks to your normal tmux
server, so it sees the same sessions as any other login.

Configuration is by environment variables (see install.sh / README):
    SERVERJACK_LISTEN How THIS process listens (ttyd is always on a Unix
                      socket). "tcp" (default) binds 127.0.0.1:$SERVERJACK_PORT
                      and refuses any connection whose other end is not owned
                      by root (tailscaled) or by us -- see peer_uid() below.
                      "unix" binds $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/serverjack/web.sock
                      (fallback base /tmp/serverjack-$UID), mode 0600, which
                      the kernel enforces instead; that mode needs `tailscale
                      serve` to be run as root once, because tailscale will not
                      proxy to a Unix socket for an unprivileged caller.
    SERVERJACK_PORT   listen port (127.0.0.1, tcp mode only) default 7680
    SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS
                      extra uids allowed to connect in tcp mode, comma-
                      separated -- a reverse proxy running as its own user
                      (nginx is often uid 101 or 33).
    SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL
                      set to 1 (or yes/true/on -- nothing else counts) to turn
                      the peer-uid check off entirely. Only if you know why: it
                      hands every local account a shell.
    SERVERJACK_TRUST_IDENTITY_UIDS
                      extra uids whose Tailscale-User-Login header is believed,
                      comma-separated. root (tailscaled) always is. Every other
                      peer's copy of that header is ignored, because a peer we
                      merely allow through could have written it itself. Only
                      list a proxy that authenticates the user and strips the
                      client's own copy.
    SERVERJACK_HOSTS  extra Host: values to answer to, comma-separated
                      (term.example.com, ...). 127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1],
                      this machine's hostname and its tailnet DNS name are
                      always accepted; anything else gets a 421, so a name
                      someone else controls cannot be pointed at this port and
                      then scripted from a browser (DNS rebinding).
    SERVERJACK_ALLOW  comma-separated tailnet logins (alice@github, ...).
                      When set, every request -- the terminal included, since
                      that is proxied through here -- must carry a
                      Tailscale-User-Login header naming one of them or it gets
                      a 403 page. The header is `tailscale serve`'s, and is
                      only read when the connection's owner is trusted to have
                      set it (see SERVERJACK_TRUST_IDENTITY_UIDS). Unset = no
                      check: every tailnet device that reaches the port gets a
                      shell.
    SERVERJACK_TITLE  page title                             default hostname
    SERVERJACK_DIRS   colon-separated dirs offered for new sessions
    SERVERJACK_TOOLS  comma-separated agent ids to show, in that order
                      (default: every tool in the registry below)
    SERVERJACK_TERM   URL path this app serves the terminal on  default /term/
    SERVERJACK_SSH    user@host for the "open in an SSH app" menu item,
                      or "off" to hide it   (default: the tailnet name)
    SERVERJACK_CONFIG config dir  (default ~/.config/serverjack)
    SERVERJACK_AUTOSTART_DELAY
                      seconds to wait after startup before running
                      autostart.json                            default 15

Runtime dir: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/serverjack (fallback /tmp/serverjack-$UID/
serverjack), created 0700 by whichever of serverjack / serverjack-ttyd starts
first, and refused if it is not a real directory owned by us with mode 0700 --
see safe_dir(). It holds ttyd.sock always, and web.sock in unix mode.

ttyd has no idea who is on the other end of its socket and no notion of a user,
so it is never reachable directly: it binds ttyd.sock inside that 0700
directory, and everything under SERVERJACK_TERM is proxied to it by this
process, after the peer-uid, Host and SERVERJACK_ALLOW checks have run. One
listener, one door, one set of checks.

Agents come from BUILTIN_TOOLS below. To change one, or add your own, drop a
JSON *list* in $SERVERJACK_CONFIG/tools.json; entries are merged over the
built-in tool with the same id (field by field, except "server"/"daemon",
which replace wholesale), "hidden": true drops one, and unknown ids are
appended. Fields: id, label, bin, paths, docs, install, install_note, needs ("node"),
login, login_note, login_check, run, run_note, server{label,cmd,session,note,per_dir},
daemon{label,start,stop,pidfile,note}, actions[{label,cmd,note,dir}], hidden.
server.per_dir: true means one server per project directory (Claude's model):
each Start makes another tmux session named <session>-<dirname> and the card
lists every instance with its directory. Otherwise there is one session.
run_note, actions[].note, server.note and daemon.note are the one-line
explanations shown beside those options; keep them to a single sentence.
"paths" lists extra directories to look for "bin" in -- a coding CLI often
installs into a private bin dir that only your .bashrc adds to PATH; the ones
that exist are appended to the PATH used for the installed/login checks, the
daemon commands and every session serverjack starts. Shell globs are allowed
("~/.local/share/pnpm/*/bin"). nvm's node directories are always appended
too, so a CLI installed with `npm install -g` under nvm is found without any
configuration.
Only id, label and bin are required. Example:

    [
      {"id": "opencode", "server": {"label": "Server for the mobile app",
        "cmd": "opencode serve --hostname 0.0.0.0", "session": "opencode-serve"}},
      {"id": "gemini", "hidden": true},
      {"id": "aider", "label": "Aider", "bin": "aider", "run": "aider",
       "install": "pipx install aider-chat"}
    ]

Every agent card submits one form to POST /tools, and the button that was
pressed carries the route in its value ("/tools/open", "/tools/action:0"...),
so a card can share one directory picker between several buttons. Those same
routes also work as plain endpoints of their own.

Security: no password of its own. Every button on this page runs a command as
the user serverjack runs as -- it is SSH with a nicer font. Only expose it
through something that authenticates (tailscale serve, an SSO reverse proxy, a
VPN); never `tailscale funnel` it. Other accounts on the same machine are kept
out two ways: in tcp mode every connection's owner is looked up in
/proc/net/tcp and refused unless it is root, us, or a uid in
SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS; in unix mode the socket is 0600 inside a 0700 directory
and the kernel does it. The terminal is behind the same door, because it is
this process that serves it. Identity, when you want it, comes from Tailscale:
SERVERJACK_ALLOW names the tailnet logins allowed in. Requests naming a Host we
do not recognise get a 421 (DNS rebinding), and state-changing POSTs are
refused unless the browser says they came from this same site (Sec-Fetch-Site /
Origin -- neither present is also a refusal), so a random web page can't drive
it from a logged-in browser.

Two GET routes are exempt from SERVERJACK_ALLOW: /healthz and /api/status.
Both are for machines, not people, and a dashboard tile fetching them carries
no tailnet identity of its own -- the request arrives from tailscaled (or your
proxy) with no Tailscale-User-Login header, so an ALLOW check could only ever
403 it. They are still behind the peer-uid check, so only root, you, and any
uid in SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS can ask; and /api/status deliberately reports
counts, never session names or directories, so it says nothing a health check
doesn't already leak.
"""

import concurrent.futures
import hashlib
import html
import json
import os
import re
import getpass
import glob
import shlex
import shutil
import socket
import socketserver
import stat
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time
from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer
from urllib.parse import parse_qs, quote, unquote, urlparse

# Bump with every release; the git tag for that release must read "v" + this
# (v1.0.0, v1.1.0, ...) -- install.sh --version and `--check`'s output both
# come from here, and it's what /api/status reports for a dashboard tile.
VERSION = "1.3.0"
HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
# The checkout this file lives in: <repo>/bin/serverjack. Used for the built-in
# "Update serverjack" shortcut, which only appears when <repo>/.git exists (a
# git-checkout install) or this process is physically running out of
# ~/.local/share/serverjack/releases/ (a managed, bootstrap-installed
# release) -- see _install_channel() below.
REPO = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))


def _install_channel():
    """("release", version) for a managed install (bin/serverjack-ctl,
    bootstrap/serverjack-bootstrap.sh.in) -- read once at import, since the
    answer cannot change without this unit restarting (serverjack-ctl update
    always restarts it). ("git", None) for a plain git checkout. Otherwise
    ("unknown", None): a tarball copy someone extracted by hand, or anything
    else this process doesn't recognize -- update_available() is False.

    Decided by WHERE THIS FILE PHYSICALLY LIVES first (realpath(REPO) under
    ~/.local/share/serverjack/releases/), not by whether install.json
    happens to exist -- install.json is consulted only for the version
    number to report, once the channel is already known. Getting this
    backwards misclassifies a plain git checkout as "release" on any account
    that also has an unrelated managed install sitting in
    ~/.local/share/serverjack (install.json existing there says nothing
    about which install THIS running process is)."""
    releases_root = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(HOME, ".local", "share", "serverjack", "releases"))
    if REPO == releases_root or REPO.startswith(releases_root + os.sep):
        install_json = os.path.join(HOME, ".local", "share", "serverjack", "install.json")
        try:
            with open(install_json, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                doc = json.load(fh)
            # Valid JSON but not an object (a bare number, string, list, ...)
            # is a corrupted install.json, not one this process wrote -- treat
            # it the same as missing/unreadable rather than crashing this
            # process at import (doc.get() below would otherwise raise
            # AttributeError, which is NOT one of the exceptions caught here).
            if isinstance(doc, dict) and doc.get("channel") == "release":
                return "release", doc.get("version")
        except (OSError, ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            pass
        # install.json is missing, unreadable, or does not (yet) name this
        # version -- e.g. mid-bootstrap, or a tarball extracted by hand into
        # releases/ -- fall back to the RELEASE file this release shipped
        # with, which lives right next to this file regardless.
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(REPO, "RELEASE"), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                for line in fh:
                    if line.startswith("version="):
                        return "release", line.strip().split("=", 1)[1] or None
        except OSError:
            pass
        return "release", None
    if os.path.exists(os.path.join(REPO, ".git")):
        return "git", None
    return "unknown", None


CHANNEL, _INSTALL_JSON_VERSION = _install_channel()
LISTEN = (os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_LISTEN") or "tcp").strip().lower()
if LISTEN not in ("tcp", "unix"):
    raise SystemExit("serverjack: SERVERJACK_LISTEN must be tcp or unix")
try:
    PORT = int(os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_PORT", "7680"))
except ValueError:
    raise SystemExit("serverjack: SERVERJACK_PORT must be an integer from 1 to 65535")
if not 1 <= PORT <= 65535:
    raise SystemExit("serverjack: SERVERJACK_PORT must be an integer from 1 to 65535")
BIND = ("127.0.0.1", PORT)


def safe_dir(path, what):
    """A directory that must be ours before we put a socket in it. Fails
    closed: if another local account owns the path, or can write it (mode not
    0700), or it is a symlink pointing somewhere else, then anything we bind
    inside it is theirs to intercept -- so refuse to start rather than serve a
    terminal through it. The mode alone is fixed when the owner is right."""
    try:
        st = os.lstat(path)
    except OSError as e:
        raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: cannot stat {what} {path}: {e}")
    if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
        raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: {what} {path} is a symlink -- refusing to use it")
    if not stat.S_ISDIR(st.st_mode):
        raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: {what} {path} is not a directory -- refusing to use it")
    if st.st_uid != os.getuid():
        raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: {what} {path} is owned by uid {st.st_uid}, "
                         f"not {os.getuid()} -- refusing to use it")
    if stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode) != 0o700:
        try:
            os.chmod(path, 0o700)
            mode = stat.S_IMODE(os.lstat(path).st_mode)
        except OSError as e:
            raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: {what} {path} is mode "
                             f"{stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode):04o}, not 0700, and cannot be fixed: {e}")
        if mode != 0o700:
            raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: {what} {path} is mode {mode:04o}, not 0700")
    return path


def runtime_dir():
    """Where the sockets live. Same expression as bin/serverjack-ttyd and
    bin/tmux-attach.sh use in shell:
        ${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp/serverjack-$(id -u)}/serverjack
    Not a systemd RuntimeDirectory=: that would delete the directory (and the
    other unit's socket) whenever one of the two units stops.

    The /tmp fallback is the dangerous one -- /tmp is world-writable, so a
    local attacker who wins the race to create /tmp/serverjack-<uid> would own
    the directory ttyd's socket goes in. Both it and the serverjack/ directory
    under it are therefore created 0700 and re-checked afterwards."""
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR")
    if not base:
        base = f"/tmp/serverjack-{os.getuid()}"
        try:
            os.mkdir(base, 0o700)
        except FileExistsError:
            pass
        except OSError as e:
            raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: cannot create {base}: {e}")
        safe_dir(base, "runtime dir parent")
    d = os.path.join(base, "serverjack")
    try:
        os.mkdir(d, 0o700)
    except FileExistsError:
        pass
    except OSError as e:
        raise SystemExit(f"serverjack: cannot create {d}: {e}")
    return safe_dir(d, "runtime dir")


RUNTIME_DIR = runtime_dir()
WEB_SOCK = os.path.join(RUNTIME_DIR, "web.sock")
TTYD_SOCK = os.path.join(RUNTIME_DIR, "ttyd.sock")


def env_flag(name):
    """A boolean environment variable. Strict on purpose: only the four words
    that plainly mean yes turn a safety check off, so SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL=
    False (or =off, or a typo) leaves it ON rather than silently disabling it."""
    return (os.environ.get(name) or "").strip().lower() in ("1", "yes", "true", "on")


ALLOW =[x.strip().lower() for x in (os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_ALLOW") or "").split(",") if x.strip()]

# --- who else on this machine may connect (tcp mode only) -------------------
#
# A localhost TCP port has no owner: any Linux account on the box can open
# 127.0.0.1:7680 and press the buttons, which is a shell as us. The kernel does
# record who owns each socket, though, so before routing anything we look the
# peer up in /proc/net/tcp and refuse a stranger. Allowed by default: root
# (that is tailscaled proxying for `tailscale serve`) and our own uid.
# SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS adds more -- a reverse proxy running as its own user.
# SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL=1 turns the whole thing off; see the README.
# In unix mode none of this runs: the 0700 directory already says it.

TRUST_LOCAL = env_flag("SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL")
TRUST_UIDS = {int(x) for x in re.findall(r"\d+", os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS") or "")}
ALLOWED_UIDS = {0, os.getuid()} | TRUST_UIDS
# Whose Tailscale-User-Login header we believe. root is tailscaled, which is
# what `tailscale serve` proxies through and the only thing that authenticates
# a tailnet user. Every other allowed peer -- you, a proxy uid from
# SERVERJACK_TRUST_UIDS -- can set that header to anything, so unless it is
# named here its header is treated as absent. Add a proxy's uid only when that
# proxy authenticates the user itself and strips any client-supplied copy.
TRUST_IDENTITY_UIDS = {0} | {
    int(x) for x in re.findall(r"\d+", os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TRUST_IDENTITY_UIDS") or "")}
_warned_no_row = False


def _proc_addr(field):
    """"0100007F:1E08" -> ("127.0.0.1", 7680). Each 4-byte word of the address
    is little-endian; tcp6 rows are four such words, and an IPv4-mapped peer
    (::ffff:127.0.0.1) is normalised back to its IPv4 form so both files
    compare against the same thing."""
    hexip, _, hexport = field.partition(":")
    raw = bytes.fromhex(hexip)
    raw = b"".join(raw[i:i + 4][::-1] for i in range(0, len(raw), 4))
    fam = socket.AF_INET if len(raw) == 4 else socket.AF_INET6
    ip = socket.inet_ntop(fam, raw)
    if ip.startswith("::ffff:"):
        ip = ip[7:]
    return ip, int(hexport, 16)


def _norm(ip):
    return ip[7:] if ip.startswith("::ffff:") else ip


def peer_uid(conn):
    """uid owning the other end of this connection, or None if we can't tell.
    The peer's own row is the one whose local address is our remote address and
    whose remote address is our local one."""
    try:
        peer = conn.getpeername()
        ours = conn.getsockname()
    except OSError:
        return None
    want_local = (_norm(peer[0]), peer[1])
    want_remote = (_norm(ours[0]), ours[1])
    for path in ("/proc/net/tcp", "/proc/net/tcp6"):
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                next(f, None)                      # header
                for line in f:
                    col = line.split()
                    if len(col) < 8:
                        continue
                    try:
                        if _proc_addr(col[1]) == want_local and _proc_addr(col[2]) == want_remote:
                            return int(col[7])
                    except (ValueError, OSError):
                        continue
        except OSError:
            continue
    return None


def conn_uid(conn):
    """uid owning the other end, whichever kind of socket this is. AF_UNIX
    answers directly (SO_PEERCRED); a TCP peer has to be looked up in
    /proc/net/tcp. Used both for the access check below and for deciding
    whether to believe a Tailscale-User-Login header."""
    if conn.family == socket.AF_UNIX:
        try:
            raw = conn.getsockopt(socket.SOL_SOCKET, socket.SO_PEERCRED,
                                  struct.calcsize("3i"))
            return struct.unpack("3i", raw)[1]
        except (OSError, AttributeError, struct.error):
            return None
    return peer_uid(conn)


def peer_allowed(uid):
    """Fails CLOSED: a peer we cannot identify is a peer we don't serve."""
    global _warned_no_row
    if LISTEN != "tcp" or TRUST_LOCAL:
        return True
    if uid is None:
        if not _warned_no_row:
            _warned_no_row = True
            sys.stderr.write(
                "serverjack: cannot find the peer's socket in /proc/net/tcp -- refusing "
                "connections it cannot identify. Set SERVERJACK_TRUST_LOCAL=1 to disable "
                "the check, or SERVERJACK_LISTEN=unix to not need it.\n")
        return False
    return uid in ALLOWED_UIDS
TITLE = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TITLE") or socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
# CRT effects: on unless SERVERJACK_FX says otherwise. The browser can still
# override either way (localStorage sj-fx), which is what the toggle writes.
FX_CLASS = "" if (os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_FX") or "").strip().lower() in (
    "0", "off", "no", "false") else "fx"
TERM_PATH = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_TERM", "/term/")
if not TERM_PATH.endswith("/"):
    TERM_PATH += "/"          # ttyd builds its ws URL relative to the page path
TERM_PREFIX = TERM_PATH.rstrip("/")           # "/term" -- what we strip
if not TERM_PREFIX:
    raise SystemExit('serverjack: SERVERJACK_TERM cannot be "/" -- it would put '
                     'ttyd in front of the whole app. Use /term/ or another subpath.')


# --- which Host headers we answer to (DNS rebinding) ------------------------
#
# A name someone else controls can be pointed at 127.0.0.1 and then loaded in
# your browser; the page is same-origin with *that* name, so the browser hands
# it our responses and lets it POST back. Nothing above stops it -- the
# connection really does come from a browser on this machine. So every request
# names the host it thinks it reached, and anything we don't recognise gets a
# 421 before routing.

def self_dns_name():
    """This machine's tailnet DNS name, lowercased, or "" if tailscale is not
    up. Cached; refreshed once lazily because serverjack can start before
    tailscaled has an answer."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["tailscale", "status", "--json"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=5)
        if r.returncode == 0:
            name = (json.loads(r.stdout).get("Self") or {}).get("DNSName", "")
            return name.rstrip(".").lower()
    except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    return ""


def _base_hosts():
    names = {"127.0.0.1", "localhost", "::1", "[::1]",
             "serverjack"}          # the Host curl sends over a Unix socket
    h = (socket.gethostname() or "").lower()
    if h:
        names.add(h)
        names.add(h.split(".")[0])
    for x in (os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_HOSTS") or "").split(","):
        x = x.strip().lower()
        if x:
            names.add(x)
    return names


ALLOWED_HOSTS = _base_hosts()
_hosts_lock = threading.Lock()
_hosts_refreshed = False
_tailnet = self_dns_name()
if _tailnet:
    ALLOWED_HOSTS.add(_tailnet)
    ALLOWED_HOSTS.add(_tailnet.split(".")[0])


def _hostname_of(header):
    """"[::1]:7680" -> "[::1]"; "example.com:443" -> "example.com"."""
    h = (header or "").strip().lower()
    if h.startswith("["):
        end = h.find("]")
        return h[:end + 1] if end != -1 else h
    return h.partition(":")[0]


def host_allowed(header):
    global _hosts_refreshed
    name = _hostname_of(header)
    if not name:
        return False                       # HTTP/1.1 requires a Host
    if name in ALLOWED_HOSTS:
        return True
    with _hosts_lock:
        if _hosts_refreshed:
            return name in ALLOWED_HOSTS   # another thread may have added it
        _hosts_refreshed = True
        found = self_dns_name()
        if found:
            ALLOWED_HOSTS.add(found)
            ALLOWED_HOSTS.add(found.split(".")[0])
    return name in ALLOWED_HOSTS


def _dirs():
    raw = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_DIRS") or "~/projects:~/src:~/code:~"
    out = []
    for d in raw.split(":"):
        d = os.path.expanduser(d.strip())
        if d and os.path.isdir(d) and d not in out:
            out.append(d)
    if HOME not in out:
        out.append(HOME)      # last, so the picker defaults to a real project dir
    return out


DIR_ROOTS = _dirs()
CONFIG_DIR = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_CONFIG") or os.path.join(
    os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or f"{HOME}/.config", "serverjack")
SHORTCUTS_FILE = os.path.join(CONFIG_DIR, "shortcuts.json")
TOOLS_FILE = os.path.join(CONFIG_DIR, "tools.json")
AUTOSTART_FILE = os.path.join(CONFIG_DIR, "autostart.json")
try:
    AUTOSTART_DELAY = float(os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_AUTOSTART_DELAY") or 15)
except ValueError:
    AUTOSTART_DELAY = 15.0


def _ssh_target():
    """user@host for 'connect over SSH instead' links. SERVERJACK_SSH overrides;
    otherwise the tailnet DNS name if tailscale is up, else the hostname."""
    t = os.environ.get("SERVERJACK_SSH", "").strip()
    if t == "off":
        return ""
    if t:
        return t
    host = ""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["tailscale", "status", "--json"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=5)
        if r.returncode == 0:
            host = (json.loads(r.stdout).get("Self") or {}).get("DNSName", "").rstrip(".")
    except (OSError, ValueError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        pass
    host = host or socket.getfqdn() or socket.gethostname()
    return f"{getpass.getuser()}@{host}"


SSH_TARGET = _ssh_target()


def ssh_cmd(name):
    return f"ssh -t {SSH_TARGET} tmux attach -t {shlex.quote(name)}" if SSH_TARGET else ""


PATH_ENV = os.environ.get("PATH") or f"{HOME}/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"


